<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Distinguish a connection, a resource and a symbol, and change databases deliberately.
</div>

**You need:** the packages, helpers and loaded practice data from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#before-you-start). Start a fresh R session
in `book-work` and run the sections in order.

## Start this chapter

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
practice <- book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
```

## Three names with different jobs

A **connection** is a logged-in DataSHIELD session. A **resource** describes how
the server opens a database. A **symbol** is an object name in that session.
Here `site` names the connection, `gibleed` selects the resource and `omop` names
the attached database handle.

```{r handle}
DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)
```

Under `site`, `omop` is the private handle created by `book_connect()`. Later,
`D_people` will name a table created by a recipe, rather than a database handle.

For an Opal study server, use the
[login block in Getting started](book-00-start.html#connect-to-your-opal-server).
Your administrator provides resource names including their project prefix.

## Use a handle explicitly

```{r explicit-handle}
knitr::kable(as.data.frame(ds.omop.table.stats("person", symbol = "omop", conns = conns)$per_site$site))
```

The request goes to GiBleed through `omop`: its person count is in the
2,690–2,694 band. Most dsOMOP calls default to `symbol = "omop"`, so you can
omit it when you have just one attached database.
`ds.omop.server.version()` is different: it reports the server's installed and
available methods, independently of any database handle.

## Switch resources

The practice helper closes the current session and opens the requested resource.
Always replace `conns` with the returned connection:

```{r switch-to-numeric}
practice <- book_switch("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
knitr::kable(as.data.frame(ds.omop.table.stats("person", conns = conns)$per_site$site))
```

The person band is now 25–29, containing Synthea27Nj's 28 people. The handle can
still be called `omop` even though its database changed. Switching does not
combine the two populations. On Opal, use the disconnect/attach pair in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#connect-to-your-opal-server) instead.

```{r switch-back}
practice <- book_switch("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
knitr::kable(as.data.frame(ds.omop.table.stats("person", conns = conns)$per_site$site))
```

The 2,690–2,694 band confirms that GiBleed is attached again. Choosing the
resource explicitly prevents running a lab analysis against its empty numeric fields.

## Exercise 1: keep two handles in one session

Attach the numeric resource as `labs`, keeping `omop` attached to GiBleed.
List the names and count people through each. Does attaching `labs` combine them?
For Opal, substitute the supplied numeric resource name.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r two-handles}
labs_session <- ds.omop.connect(resource = "numeric", symbol = "labs", conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.symbols(conns)
```

`labs` and `omop` are two distinct handles in the same session.

```{r two-counts}
handle_counts <- lapply(c("omop", "labs"), function(handle) {
  counts <- ds.omop.table.stats("person", stats = "persons",
    symbol = handle, conns = conns)$per_site$site
  data.frame(Handle = handle, counts)
})
knitr::kable(do.call(rbind, handle_counts), row.names = FALSE)
ds.omop.disconnect(symbol = "labs", conns = conns)
```

`omop` gives 2690 and `labs` gives 25. Each is a separate protected count; the
populations were not combined. Disconnecting `labs` leaves `omop` available.

</details>

## Exercise 2: diagnose a wrong name

Ask for a person count through `missing_handle`. What needs fixing: the table
name or the handle name?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r unknown-handle}
tryCatch(ds.omop.table.stats("person", symbol = "missing_handle", conns = conns),
  error = function(e) cat(conditionMessage(e), "\n"))
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

The message identifies a missing session handle. It says nothing about whether
the database has a person table. Use `omop`, or attach a resource under the name
you intend to query.

</details>

## End the session

```{r logout}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
```

Disconnect releases the database handle; logout ends the DataSHIELD session.
The next chapter opens a fresh connection.

## Recap

A resource selects a database, a connection carries requests, and a symbol names
a private server object. Check the selected resource before analysing it.

**Next:** [3. Exploring the CDM](book-03-explore.html).
